# --hide-unstaged stashes every unstaged change first, so the project-wide
# `tsc` in lint-staged.config.mts sees the tree exactly as the commit will
# leave it, rather than a mix of staged and working-tree content. No task
# writes anything git tracks, so restoring that stash cannot conflict.
#
# --fail-on-changes makes that last sentence a checked property rather than a
# claim. If a task ever does rewrite a tracked file, the commit fails and says
# so, instead of the rewrite being quietly restored over by the stash.
#
# --continue-on-error reports every failing check in one pass, rather than
# stopping at the first and making the next one a surprise.
lint-staged --hide-unstaged --fail-on-changes --continue-on-error
